MEASURING BUSINESS SIZE

Possible measures include:
• number of employees;
• capital employed;
• sales revenue/turnover;
• output;
• market share;
• asset value;
• geographical coverage.

No single measure is perfect. A technology company may have few employees but very high revenue, while a labour-intensive business may employ many workers with lower turnover.

SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES

SMEs can contribute through:
• employment creation;
• local supply chains;
• innovation;
• regional development;
• entrepreneurship;
• income generation;
• flexible response to local markets.

COMMON SME PROBLEMS
• limited finance;
• inadequate collateral;
• weak management skills;
• small customer base;
• high unit costs;
• unreliable infrastructure;
• competition from larger firms;
• limited technology;
• informal record keeping;
• difficulties accessing markets;
• regulatory burdens;
• shortages of specialised skills.

POSSIBLE SOLUTIONS
• training;
• improved bookkeeping;
• cooperative purchasing;
• digital marketing;
• formalisation;
• business incubation;
• appropriate financing;
• networking;
• quality certification;
• market diversification;
• improved inventory control.

ADVANTAGES OF SMALL BUSINESSES
• close customer relationships;
• quick decisions;
• flexibility;
• lower bureaucracy;
• niche-market focus.

DISADVANTAGES
• limited finance;
• weak bargaining power;
• dependence on owner;
• limited economies of scale;
• vulnerability to shocks.

ADVANTAGES OF LARGE BUSINESSES
• economies of scale;
• access to finance;
• specialist staff;
• stronger bargaining power;
• greater brand recognition.

DISADVANTAGES
• bureaucracy;
• slower communication;
• diseconomies of scale;
• difficult coordination;
• less personal service.

BUSINESS GROWTH
Internal/organic growth:
Increasing capacity, products, outlets or market share using the firm's own expansion.

External growth:
Merger, takeover or other forms of integration.

HORIZONTAL INTEGRATION
Businesses at the same stage of production combine.

VERTICAL INTEGRATION
Businesses at different stages of the supply chain combine.
Backward integration moves toward suppliers.
Forward integration moves toward distribution/customers.

CONGLOMERATE/DIVERSIFICATION
Expansion into unrelated activities.

REASONS FOR GROWTH
• increased market share;
• economies of scale;
• reduced dependence on one product;
• stronger bargaining power;
• increased profits;
• defensive response to competitors.

RISKS OF GROWTH
• excessive borrowing;
• loss of control;
• integration problems;
• diseconomies;
• culture clashes;
• management overload.

BUSINESS FAILURE
Possible causes:
• poor cash-flow management;
• weak demand;
• excessive costs;
• poor pricing;
• undercapitalisation;
• fraud;
• poor management;
• unsuitable location;
• excessive competition;
• economic shocks;
• failure to adapt technology.

DISTINGUISH PROFIT FROM CASH
A business can report accounting profit and still fail because cash is tied up in inventory and receivables or because debt repayments are large.

STATE INTERVENTION
Government may support SMEs through training, infrastructure, finance schemes, procurement opportunities or policy support, while taxes, licensing and regulation may constrain them.

EXAM EVALUATION
When assessing a growth strategy, consider finance, management capacity, market demand, risk, control, integration costs and the likely effect on stakeholders.

BUSINESS AIMS
An aim is a broad statement of what an organisation seeks to achieve.

OBJECTIVES
Objectives are more specific targets used to guide action and measure progress.

SMART OBJECTIVES
Specific
Measurable
Achievable
Relevant
Time-bound

HIERARCHY
Strategic objectives are long-term and broad.
Tactical objectives translate strategy into medium-term departmental action.
Operational objectives concern shorter-term activities.

COMMON OBJECTIVES
• survival;
• profit;
• growth;
• market share;
• customer satisfaction;
• quality;
• social responsibility;
• employee welfare;
• environmental sustainability.

OBJECTIVE CONFLICT
Profit maximisation may conflict with:
• employee wage demands;
• low-price strategies;
• environmental investment;
• community spending.

Objectives may also change with circumstances. A new business may prioritise survival and cash flow, while a mature business may emphasise growth or shareholder returns.

BUSINESS ETHICS
Ethics concern principles of right and wrong behaviour in business decisions.

Examples:
• truthful advertising;
• fair treatment of employees;
• avoiding bribery;
• responsible sourcing;
• customer privacy;
• product safety;
• honest financial reporting.

WHY ETHICS MATTER
Ethical conduct can:
• build trust;
• protect reputation;
• reduce legal risk;
• improve employee commitment;
• strengthen customer loyalty.

Unethical conduct may create short-term gains but expose a business to fines, lawsuits, reputational damage and loss of trust.

CORPORATE SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY
CSR involves considering the social and environmental effects of business activity in addition to financial objectives.

Areas include:
• employee welfare;
• community investment;
• environmental protection;
• responsible sourcing;
• consumer safety;
• education and skills development.

CSR BENEFITS
• reputation;
• customer loyalty;
• employee attraction;
• stakeholder relationships;
• risk reduction;
• licence to operate.

CSR COSTS/CRITICISMS
• higher short-run costs;
• possible diversion of funds;
• difficulty measuring social returns;
• risk of “greenwashing” or symbolic activity;
• conflict with short-term profit targets.

SUSTAINABILITY
Sustainable business seeks long-term economic viability while managing social and environmental impacts.

EXAM STRUCTURE
For “Should a business prioritise CSR over profit?”:
1. define CSR and profit;
2. explain arguments for CSR;
3. explain opportunity costs;
4. consider business size and industry;
5. distinguish short-term from long-term effects;
6. give a justified conclusion.

ICT RESOURCES
Examples:
• computers;
• smartphones;
• servers/cloud services;
• databases;
• point-of-sale systems;
• accounting software;
• enterprise systems;
• websites;
• social media;
• digital payment systems;
• communication platforms.

FUNCTIONAL USES

Finance:
Electronic records, accounting systems, budgeting and financial reporting.

Marketing:
Digital advertising, social media, customer analytics and e-commerce.

Operations:
Stock control, scheduling, automation and production monitoring.

Human resources:
Payroll, recruitment systems, training platforms and employee records.

Communication:
Email, video conferencing, instant messaging and collaboration tools.

BENEFITS
• faster communication;
• lower transaction costs;
• improved accuracy;
• better data analysis;
• wider market access;
• automation;
• improved customer service;
• easier remote work;
• improved inventory visibility.

LIMITATIONS
• acquisition cost;
• training needs;
• system downtime;
• cybersecurity risks;
• privacy concerns;
• technological obsolescence;
• dependence on electricity/connectivity;
• resistance to change.

E-COMMERCE
Buying and selling using electronic channels.

Benefits to sellers:
• wider geographical reach;
• lower physical-store dependence;
• customer data;
• 24-hour availability.

Benefits to customers:
• convenience;
• price comparison;
• broader choice.

Risks:
• fraud;
• delivery problems;
• cyberattacks;
• privacy issues;
• inability to inspect products physically.

DIGITAL PAYMENTS
Can increase transaction speed and traceability, but require secure systems, user awareness and reliable connectivity.

DATA AS A BUSINESS RESOURCE
Businesses use data to:
• forecast demand;
• segment customers;
• monitor performance;
• detect fraud;
• personalise marketing;
• improve operations.

DATA QUALITY
Useful data should be accurate, relevant, timely, complete and appropriately protected.

CYBERSECURITY
Threats include:
• phishing;
• malware;
• password attacks;
• unauthorised access;
• data theft;
• ransomware.

Controls:
• access controls;
• backups;
• staff training;
• multi-factor authentication;
• security updates;
• encryption where appropriate;
• incident-response procedures.

DIGITAL TRANSFORMATION
Transformation is more than buying computers. It involves redesigning processes, skills and business models around digital capabilities.

EXAM APPLICATION
When evaluating ICT investment, compare:
initial cost + training + maintenance + security
against
labour savings + higher revenue + better quality + faster service + improved information.

BUSINESS PLAN

A business plan is a structured document explaining the proposed business, market, operations, management and financial requirements.

PURPOSES
• guide the entrepreneur;
• communicate the idea to investors/lenders;
• identify risks;
• set objectives;
• coordinate activities;
• establish financial requirements;
• provide benchmarks for performance.

EXECUTIVE SUMMARY
Summarises the key proposal:
• business idea;
• target market;
• competitive advantage;
• management;
• funding requirement;
• expected outcomes.

ORGANISATIONAL BACKGROUND
May include:
• ownership;
• legal form;
• location;
• mission;
• history where applicable.

PRODUCT/SERVICE
Explain:
• customer need;
• features;
• benefits;
• quality;
• differentiation;
• intellectual property considerations.

MARKET ANALYSIS
Study:
• market size;
• target customers;
• competitors;
• demand;
• trends;
• pricing;
• barriers to entry.

MARKETING STRATEGY
Set out:
• positioning;
• product;
• price;
• promotion;
• place/distribution.

OPERATIONS PLAN
Explain:
• premises;
• suppliers;
• production process;
• equipment;
• technology;
• capacity;
• quality control;
• inventory.

MANAGEMENT PLAN
Identify:
• roles;
• responsibilities;
• skills;
• staffing requirements;
• reporting relationships.

FINANCIAL PLAN
May include:
• start-up costs;
• sales forecast;
• cash budget;
• projected income statement;
• projected statement of financial position;
• break-even analysis;
• funding requirements.

PROJECT DEVELOPMENT
Project formulation converts an idea into a planned sequence of activities.

STAGES
1. Identify the problem/opportunity.
2. Generate alternatives.
3. Select the project.
4. Define objectives.
5. Conduct feasibility analysis.
6. Prepare proposal.
7. Prepare schedule and budget.
8. Implement.
9. Monitor.
10. Evaluate.

FEASIBILITY
Market feasibility — is there sufficient demand?
Technical feasibility — can the product/service be produced?
Financial feasibility — is funding and cash flow adequate?
Legal feasibility — can it operate within regulations?
Operational feasibility — can the organisation deliver consistently?

RISK REGISTER
For each risk identify:
• probability;
• impact;
• owner;
• preventive action;
• contingency.

EXAM TIP
A business plan is not a guarantee of success. Forecasts depend on assumptions. Strong plans state assumptions and include sensitivity/risk analysis.

LEADERSHIP

Leadership is the ability to influence people toward organisational goals.

QUALITIES OF EFFECTIVE LEADERS
• communication;
• integrity;
• confidence;
• adaptability;
• emotional intelligence;
• decisiveness;
• vision;
• accountability;
• problem-solving ability.

LEADERSHIP STYLES

AUTOCRATIC
Leader makes decisions with limited subordinate participation.
Useful where:
• decisions are urgent;
• workers are inexperienced;
• strict control is necessary.
Risks:
• low participation;
• resentment;
• dependence on leader.

DEMOCRATIC/PARTICIPATIVE
Employees contribute to decisions.
Benefits:
• commitment;
• ideas;
• motivation.
Limitations:
• slower decisions;
• unsuitable in emergencies.

LAISSEZ-FAIRE
Employees receive substantial autonomy.
Can work with highly skilled, self-motivated teams.
Can fail where employees need direction.

SITUATIONAL LEADERSHIP
Style changes according to circumstances and follower readiness.

THEORY X AND THEORY Y
Theory X assumes employees may dislike work and require close control.
Theory Y assumes work can be natural and people can be self-directed when conditions support commitment.

INFORMAL LEADERSHIP
Influence may emerge from expertise, personality, trust or group respect rather than formal authority.

ROLE OF WORKER REPRESENTATIVES
They can:
• communicate employee concerns;
• negotiate with management;
• assist conflict resolution;
• represent workers collectively.

LEADERSHIP IN CHANGE
Effective leaders should:
• explain why change is necessary;
• communicate clearly;
• involve affected employees;
• provide training;
• manage resistance;
• monitor outcomes.

EXAM APPLICATION
If a factory introduces new technology, a participative approach may improve acceptance because employees can raise concerns and contribute implementation ideas. However, urgent safety changes may require more directive leadership.

MOTIVATION

Motivation is the willingness of employees to direct effort toward organisational objectives.

WHY MOTIVATION MATTERS
• productivity;
• quality;
• attendance;
• retention;
• creativity;
• customer service;
• organisational commitment.

MASLOW'S HIERARCHY
Commonly presented as:
1. physiological needs;
2. safety;
3. social belonging;
4. esteem;
5. self-actualisation.

Business implication:
Managers should recognise that pay may address basic needs but may not by itself satisfy higher-level needs.

HERZBERG
Hygiene factors include pay, working conditions, company policy and supervision. Their absence can cause dissatisfaction.
Motivators include achievement, recognition, responsibility and advancement.

TAYLOR
Emphasised financial incentives and efficiency, particularly in repetitive work.

MAYO
Highlighted social relationships, group influence and attention to employees.

FINANCIAL METHODS
• wages/salary;
• bonuses;
• commission;
• profit sharing;
• performance-related pay;
• overtime pay.

NON-FINANCIAL METHODS
• job enrichment;
• job enlargement;
• recognition;
• promotion;
• training;
• participation;
• flexible work;
• improved working conditions;
• responsibility.

JOB ENRICHMENT
Adds greater responsibility, autonomy and meaningfulness.

JOB ENLARGEMENT
Adds tasks at a similar level.

JOB ROTATION
Moves employees among tasks to increase variety and skills.

EVALUATING REWARD SYSTEMS
Consider:
• cost;
• fairness;
• measurable performance;
• employee preferences;
• risk of unhealthy competition;
• quality effects;
• long-term motivation.

PERFORMANCE PAY RISK
If workers are rewarded only for quantity, quality or safety may decline. Good systems align rewards with the organisation's full objectives.

EXAM CONCLUSION
No motivation theory explains every worker. The best approach depends on job design, employee needs, organisational culture and available resources.

PURPOSES
Communication enables businesses to:
• give instructions;
• coordinate activities;
• exchange information;
• motivate;
• negotiate;
• persuade;
• report performance;
• manage stakeholders.

DIRECTIONS
Downward — management to employees.
Upward — employees to management.
Horizontal — between colleagues/departments.
Diagonal — across levels/functions.

FORMAL COMMUNICATION
Uses established organisational channels.

INFORMAL COMMUNICATION
Uses unofficial social networks and relationships. It can be fast but may spread inaccurate information.

METHODS
Oral:
Meetings, telephone, presentations.

Written:
Letters, reports, notices, minutes.

Visual:
Charts, diagrams, dashboards.

Digital:
Email, messaging, video conferencing, collaboration systems.

CHOOSING A METHOD
Consider:
• urgency;
• complexity;
• confidentiality;
• cost;
• audience;
• need for permanent record;
• opportunity for feedback.

BARRIERS
• language;
• noise;
• poor listening;
• information overload;
• status differences;
• cultural differences;
• technology failure;
• unclear messages;
• assumptions;
• lack of feedback.

EFFECTIVE COMMUNICATION
Use:
• clear purpose;
• appropriate language;
• concise structure;
• accurate data;
• suitable channel;
• feedback;
• active listening.

MEETINGS
Good meetings require:
• agenda;
• appropriate participants;
• preparation;
• time management;
• clear decisions;
• minutes;
• assigned actions.

NEGOTIATION
Effective negotiation aims for acceptable outcomes while protecting relationships. Preparation should identify objectives, alternatives, constraints and bargaining points.

MANAGEMENT'S ROLE
Managers should:
• establish channels;
• encourage upward feedback;
• prevent information bottlenecks;
• clarify responsibilities;
• use technology appropriately;
• model respectful communication.

EXAM APPLICATION
If communication is failing between sales and production, diagnose the specific barrier first. A new communication platform will not solve a problem caused by unclear responsibilities or poor information quality.

THE 4Ps

PRODUCT
Decisions include:
• design;
• quality;
• features;
• branding;
• packaging;
• product range;
• warranties;
• after-sales service.

PRODUCT LIFE CYCLE
Introduction — awareness building and market entry.
Growth — rising sales and competition.
Maturity — slower growth and strong competition.
Decline — falling demand unless renewed.

Extension strategies:
• new features;
• new packaging;
• new markets;
• promotional changes;
• repositioning.

PRICE
Methods include:
• cost-plus;
• competitive pricing;
• penetration;
• price skimming;
• promotional pricing;
• psychological pricing;
• value-based pricing.

Penetration:
Low introductory price to gain market share quickly.

Skimming:
High initial price to capture customers willing to pay more, often where differentiation or innovation exists.

PROMOTION
• advertising;
• sales promotion;
• public relations;
• personal selling;
• direct marketing;
• digital/social media.

PLACE
Distribution decisions:
• direct selling;
• retailers;
• wholesalers;
• agents;
• online channels.

PROMOTIONAL MIX
The best mix depends on:
• target market;
• product type;
• budget;
• buying process;
• competition;
• stage of product life cycle.

7Ps
For service businesses, additional elements often include:
• people;
• process;
• physical evidence.

MARKETING STRATEGY
A coherent strategy aligns:
target market + positioning + product + price + promotion + distribution.

POSITIONING
How customers perceive the brand relative to competitors.

BRANDING
A strong brand can create recognition, trust and differentiation, but requires consistent quality and communication.

INTEGRATED MARKETING
Messages across channels should be consistent while adapted to each medium.

DIGITAL MARKETING
Benefits:
• precise targeting;
• measurable engagement;
• rapid feedback;
• lower entry barriers.

Risks:
• negative reviews spread quickly;
• privacy concerns;
• algorithm changes;
• content saturation.

EXAM EVALUATION
A marketing mix should fit the market. A low-price strategy may be inappropriate for a premium brand, while high promotion cannot compensate indefinitely for poor product quality.

OPERATIONS

Operations transforms inputs into outputs.

INPUTS
• raw materials;
• labour;
• capital;
• information;
• energy.

TRANSFORMATION
Activities that convert inputs into goods/services.

OUTPUTS
Products, services and waste/by-products.

OPERATIONS OBJECTIVES
• cost;
• quality;
• speed;
• dependability;
• flexibility;
• sustainability.

PRODUCTION METHODS

JOB PRODUCTION
One-off or customised products.
Advantages:
• high customisation;
• suitable for unique orders.
Disadvantages:
• high unit cost;
• skilled labour;
• difficult scheduling.

BATCH PRODUCTION
Groups of identical products are made before switching to another batch.
Benefits:
• some economies of scale;
• flexibility.
Problems:
• setup/changeover time;
• work-in-progress.

FLOW/MASS PRODUCTION
Large quantities of standardised output.
Benefits:
• high productivity;
• low unit costs.
Risks:
• inflexibility;
• high capital requirement;
• breakdown can disrupt the whole line.

CONTINUOUS PRODUCTION
Highly automated, continuous processes, often in process industries.

CAPACITY
Maximum output that can be produced under specified conditions.

CAPACITY UTILISATION
Actual output / maximum possible output × 100.

Example:
Actual output 8,000 units; capacity 10,000.
Utilisation = 80%.

Low utilisation can mean spare capacity but may increase unit fixed costs. Very high utilisation can create congestion, overtime and maintenance problems.

PRODUCTIVITY
Output / input.

Labour productivity:
Output / labour input.

EFFICIENCY
Producing desired output with minimal waste of resources.

LEAN OPERATIONS
Focuses on eliminating waste, improving flow and continuous improvement.

COMMON WASTES
• overproduction;
• waiting;
• unnecessary transport;
• excess processing;
• inventory;
• motion;
• defects;
• underused talent.

AUTOMATION
Can improve speed and consistency but requires capital, skills and maintenance.

OUTSOURCING
Benefits:
• access to specialist expertise;
• lower fixed costs;
• focus on core activities.
Risks:
• quality control;
• dependence;
• confidentiality;
• supply disruption.

EXAM EVALUATION
Choose production method based on volume, variety, customisation, capital, skill requirements and demand stability.

INVENTORY TYPES
• raw materials;
• work in progress;
• finished goods;
• consumables/spares.

WHY HOLD INVENTORY
• avoid stockouts;
• support continuous production;
• meet unexpected demand;
• gain purchasing economies;
• buffer supply uncertainty.

COSTS OF HOLDING INVENTORY
• storage;
• insurance;
• security;
• deterioration;
• obsolescence;
• tied-up working capital.

STOCKOUT COSTS
• lost sales;
• production stoppages;
• customer dissatisfaction;
• emergency purchasing.

REORDER LEVEL
A stock level at which a new order should be placed.

REORDER QUANTITY
Amount ordered when replenishing stock.

LEAD TIME
Time between placing an order and receiving it.

BUFFER/SAFETY STOCK
Additional inventory held against uncertainty.

STOCK CONTROL CHART
Can show:
• maximum level;
• minimum level;
• reorder level;
• usage;
• lead time.

ECONOMIC ORDER QUANTITY
EOQ seeks an order size that balances ordering and holding costs under specified assumptions.

A common formula is:
EOQ = √(2DS/H)
where:
D = annual demand,
S = ordering cost per order,
H = annual holding cost per unit.

Example:
D = 10,000 units
S = $50
H = $2
EOQ = √(2×10,000×50/2)
= √500,000
≈ 707 units.

JUST-IN-TIME
Inventory arrives close to when needed.

Benefits:
• lower holding costs;
• less obsolete stock;
• less storage.

Risks:
• supplier disruption;
• transport delays;
• demand uncertainty;
• little buffer stock.

ABC ANALYSIS
Classify inventory by importance/value so management attention is concentrated on critical items.

STOCK RECORDS
Perpetual systems update balances after transactions. Periodic systems determine stock at intervals.

INVENTORY ACCURACY
Use:
• physical counts;
• reconciliation;
• authorised adjustments;
• barcode/RFID systems where appropriate;
• segregation of duties.

EXAM TIP
When recommending a stock policy, balance holding cost against stockout risk rather than saying “keep inventory as low as possible.”

CASH FLOW

Cash inflow:
Money entering the business.

Cash outflow:
Money leaving the business.

Net cash flow:
Cash inflows − cash outflows.

Closing cash:
Opening cash + net cash flow.

CASH-FLOW FORECAST
Forecast timing of:
• sales receipts;
• purchases;
• wages;
• rent;
• tax;
• loan repayments;
• capital expenditure.

CREDIT SALES
A business must distinguish sales revenue from cash collected.

Example:
January credit sales = $50,000.
70% collected in January, 30% in February.
January cash collection = $35,000.
February collection from January = $15,000.

WORKING CAPITAL CYCLE
Cash → inventory → sales → receivables → cash.

A long cycle ties up funds.

RECEIVABLE MANAGEMENT
• credit checks;
• clear credit terms;
• prompt invoicing;
• statements;
• collection follow-up;
• appropriate incentives for early payment;
• control of overdue accounts.

PAYABLE MANAGEMENT
Longer supplier credit can support liquidity, but deliberate late payment can damage reputation and supply relationships.

INVENTORY MANAGEMENT
Excess inventory ties up cash; insufficient inventory risks lost sales or production stoppage.

WAYS TO IMPROVE CASH FLOW
• accelerate collections;
• negotiate supplier terms;
• reduce excess inventory;
• delay non-essential expenditure;
• improve pricing/margins;
• sell surplus assets;
• refinance expensive short-term debt where appropriate.

OVERTRADING
Rapid growth can create a cash shortage because the business must finance inventory, receivables and capacity before receiving enough cash from customers.

WARNING SIGNS
• rising sales but falling cash;
• increasing receivables;
• increasing overdraft;
• inability to pay suppliers on time;
• rapid inventory growth.

CASH BUDGET INTERPRETATION
A negative monthly cash balance indicates a funding requirement, not necessarily an accounting loss.

EXAM APPLICATION
If a business has strong demand but weak cash flow, recommend controls around credit, inventory and growth financing rather than simply “increase sales.”

INTERNATIONAL TRADE

International trade is exchange of goods/services across national borders.

IMPORTS
Goods/services bought from abroad.

EXPORTS
Goods/services sold abroad.

BENEFITS TO BUSINESSES
• larger markets;
• access to resources;
• economies of scale;
• diversification;
• access to technology;
• foreign-currency earnings.

RISKS
• exchange-rate volatility;
• transport costs;
• customs procedures;
• political risk;
• different regulations;
• cultural differences;
• overseas competition;
• payment risk.

EXCHANGE RATES
If a Zimbabwean business imports goods priced in foreign currency, a depreciation of the local currency can increase the local-currency cost of imports.

Exporters may benefit from a weaker local currency if their foreign-currency prices remain competitive, but imported inputs can reduce that advantage.

TRADE BARRIERS
• tariffs;
• quotas;
• licensing;
• standards;
• embargoes/restrictions;
• customs procedures.

FREE TRADE
Reduces barriers to trade.

PROTECTIONISM
Uses barriers to protect domestic industries.

ARGUMENTS FOR PROTECTION
• infant-industry protection;
• employment;
• strategic industries;
• national security;
• response to unfair trade practices.

ARGUMENTS AGAINST
• higher prices;
• reduced competition;
• inefficiency;
• retaliation;
• reduced consumer choice.

GLOBALISATION
Increasing integration of markets, production and business activities across countries.

MULTINATIONAL ENTERPRISES
Businesses operating in more than one country.

BENEFITS TO HOST ECONOMIES
• employment;
• investment;
• technology transfer;
• export development.

POTENTIAL CONCERNS
• environmental effects;
• labour standards;
• profit repatriation;
• market dominance;
• dependence.

CROSS-CULTURAL MANAGEMENT
Businesses must adapt communication, products and negotiation styles to local cultural expectations without compromising legal and ethical standards.

EXAM EVALUATION
International expansion should be assessed using market potential, entry costs, exchange-rate risk, regulation, logistics, culture and the firm's capabilities.

ENTREPRENEUR

An entrepreneur identifies opportunities, organises resources, makes decisions and accepts calculated risk.

ENTREPRENEURIAL QUALITIES
• creativity;
• initiative;
• resilience;
• calculated risk-taking;
• opportunity recognition;
• communication;
• persistence;
• adaptability.

BUSINESS IDEA SOURCES
• customer problems;
• technology;
• local resources;
• unmet demand;
• demographic changes;
• import substitution opportunities;
• environmental needs.

INNOVATION
Can involve:
• new products;
• process improvements;
• business models;
• marketing methods;
• technology.

INVENTION VS INNOVATION
Invention creates something new.
Innovation applies a new idea in a useful commercial or organisational context.

RISK
Risk involves uncertainty about outcomes.

BUSINESS RISKS
• market risk;
• financial risk;
• operational risk;
• strategic risk;
• legal risk;
• reputational risk;
• cyber risk;
• environmental risk.

RISK MANAGEMENT
1. Identify.
2. Assess probability and impact.
3. Prioritise.
4. Prevent/reduce.
5. Transfer where appropriate.
6. Accept residual risk.
7. Monitor.

INSURANCE
Can transfer specified financial consequences of insured risks to an insurer, subject to policy terms.

CONTINGENCY PLANNING
Plans responses to disruptions such as:
• supplier failure;
• fire;
• cyberattack;
• power interruption;
• severe weather;
• key-person loss.

BUSINESS CONTINUITY
Focuses on maintaining critical operations during and after disruption.

INNOVATION RISKS
• development cost;
• customer rejection;
• imitation;
• technical failure;
• regulatory barriers.

FAILURE CAN PROVIDE LEARNING
Entrepreneurial learning can improve future decisions, but repeated failure without changing assumptions is not a strategy.

EXAM APPLICATION
For a proposed enterprise, combine:
opportunity analysis + customer need + resources + cost structure + risk + legal requirements + implementation plan.

CASE-STUDY METHOD

A Business Studies case question tests application, analysis and evaluation, not only definitions.

STEP 1 — IDENTIFY THE COMMAND WORD

Define:
Give the meaning.

Explain:
State the point and show how/why.

Analyse:
Break the issue into linked causes/effects.

Discuss:
Present relevant arguments from more than one side.

Evaluate:
Judge the importance using evidence and reach a reasoned conclusion.

Recommend:
Choose an option and justify it against alternatives.

STEP 2 — USE CASE EVIDENCE
If the case says:
“Sales have fallen 15% after a competitor introduced a cheaper product,”
do not write a generic paragraph about falling sales. Link the answer to:
• competitor pricing;
• price elasticity;
• differentiation;
• promotional response;
• cost structure.

STEP 3 — BUILD ANALYSIS
Point → explanation → business effect → consequence.

Example:
“Introducing digital ordering could reduce waiting time. Faster service may improve customer satisfaction, increasing repeat purchases and therefore revenue, provided the target customers have access to the technology.”

STEP 4 — EVALUATE
Use:
• short-term vs long-term;
• internal vs external factors;
• financial vs non-financial;
• stakeholder impact;
• risk;
• business size;
• available resources.

DATA RESPONSE
Read tables/graphs carefully:
• identify units;
• compare absolute and percentage changes;
• identify trends;
• avoid confusing correlation with causation.

PERCENTAGE CHANGE
(New − Old) / Old × 100.

Example:
Sales rise from $80,000 to $100,000:
20,000/80,000 ×100 = 25%.

MARKET SHARE
Firm sales / total market sales ×100.

EXAM PARAGRAPH MODEL
Point:
“Training may improve productivity.”

Application:
“The factory is introducing automated machinery.”

Analysis:
“Employees who understand the equipment are more likely to use it correctly, reducing downtime and defects.”

Evaluation:
“However, training costs are significant and benefits may take time to appear; management should prioritise training for roles most affected by the technology.”

CONCLUSION
A good conclusion:
• answers the question directly;
• uses case evidence;
• recognises conditions;
• gives a justified recommendation.

COMMON MISTAKES
• definition dumping;
• ignoring the case;
• no calculations shown;
• one-sided evaluation;
• repeating the same point;
• unsupported conclusions;
• confusing revenue, profit and cash.

BUSINESS IN ZIMBABWE

Business decisions should be analysed within the actual operating environment rather than through abstract theory alone.

AREAS TO CONSIDER
• inflation and price changes;
• exchange-rate movements;
• access to finance;
• electricity and infrastructure reliability;
• transport/logistics;
• local purchasing power;
• informal-sector competition;
• import costs;
• regional trade;
• agricultural and mineral linkages;
• technology adoption;
• regulatory requirements.

SME APPLICATION
A Zimbabwean SME may face a combination of:
limited working capital + volatile input costs + small market size + infrastructure constraints.

A useful response is not merely “get a bank loan.” Consider:
• inventory policy;
• supplier negotiations;
• pricing;
• customer credit;
• digital sales;
• product diversification;
• partnerships;
• appropriate external finance.

IMPORT DEPENDENCE
Where imported inputs are important, exchange-rate changes can affect:
• cost of goods;
• selling prices;
• margins;
• working capital requirements.

EXPORT OPPORTUNITY
Businesses may expand through regional markets where they have:
• competitive products;
• reliable capacity;
• compliant quality;
• suitable logistics;
• foreign-currency earning potential.

INFORMAL SECTOR
Informal businesses can provide employment and flexible services but may face limited access to formal finance, technology and markets. Formalisation can improve access to some opportunities while increasing compliance responsibilities.

BUSINESS ETHICS
Zimbabwean businesses, like businesses elsewhere, should consider:
• fair treatment of workers;
• customer protection;
• accurate records;
• responsible sourcing;
• anti-corruption controls;
• environmental responsibility.

HERITAGE AND COMMUNITY
Business development can use local knowledge, cultural products, skills and community networks while maintaining quality, sustainability and respect for intellectual property.

SUSTAINABILITY
A business should consider:
• efficient resource use;
• waste reduction;
• energy choices;
• climate resilience;
• responsible water use;
• community impact.

CASE-STUDY APPLICATION
When answering a Zimbabwe-based case:
1. identify the specific problem;
2. use the local context;
3. apply a relevant business theory;
4. quantify where possible;
5. evaluate realistic alternatives;
6. recommend an action consistent with the firm's resources.

CASE

A Zimbabwean clothing business has strong sales among young urban customers but weak sales in smaller towns. It has a recognised brand but limited advertising funds.

ANALYSIS

SEGMENTATION
The firm should examine:
• age;
• location;
• income;
• lifestyle;
• purchasing behaviour.

MARKET DEVELOPMENT
Selling the existing product to new geographical markets is market development under the Ansoff framework.

RESEARCH
Use secondary data first to identify market size and competitors, followed by targeted primary research to understand local preferences.

DISTRIBUTION
Options include:
• local retailers;
• agents;
• mobile/social commerce;
• direct delivery;
• partnerships.

PROMOTION
Limited funds favour measurable, targeted digital promotion and local partnerships rather than an expensive mass campaign.

PRICE
The business should assess local purchasing power and competitors. A lower price may increase volume but could weaken margins and brand positioning.

PRODUCT
The firm may need localised sizes, designs or product bundles if research shows genuine differences in demand.

MARKETING MIX RECOMMENDATION
Do not change all 4Ps simultaneously without evidence. Identify the biggest constraint first.

MEASUREMENT
Track:
• sales by region;
• conversion rate;
• repeat purchase;
• customer acquisition cost;
• average transaction value;
• market share.

EVALUATION
A successful urban brand is not automatically successful in another location. The firm must test assumptions through research and a controlled market launch.

CASE

A manufacturer frequently runs out of raw materials while also holding large quantities of slow-moving finished goods.

DIAGNOSIS
The business has an inventory-management problem rather than simply an inventory shortage.

RAW MATERIALS
Stockouts suggest:
• poor forecasting;
• long lead times;
• weak reorder systems;
• unreliable suppliers.

FINISHED GOODS
High slow-moving stock suggests:
• overproduction;
• inaccurate demand forecasts;
• poor product mix;
• weak sales planning.

SOLUTIONS
• classify inventory;
• calculate appropriate reorder levels;
• review supplier lead times;
• improve demand forecasts;
• align production with sales;
• introduce inventory ageing reports;
• investigate obsolete stock.

WORKING CAPITAL EFFECT
Excess finished goods tie up cash and may require discounting. Raw-material stockouts can reduce production and sales.

FINANCE CHOICE
If the problem is temporary, short-term working-capital finance may help, but borrowing does not solve inefficient stock control. Long-term borrowing should not be used merely to finance a permanent working-capital weakness without a credible improvement plan.

QUALITY
Reducing inventory should not lead to unreliable production or poor quality. The business must balance cost, service and resilience.

OPERATIONS KPI
• stock turnover;
• stockout frequency;
• inventory days;
• defect rate;
• production downtime;
• capacity utilisation;
• order fulfilment time.

CONCLUSION
The priority should be operational control and forecasting, followed by appropriately sized finance if a temporary liquidity gap remains.

SCENARIO 1: INFLATION

A manufacturer experiences a sharp increase in input prices.

Possible effects:
• variable costs rise;
• contribution per unit falls if selling price is unchanged;
• working-capital requirements may increase;
• customers may reduce purchases if prices rise;
• suppliers may demand faster payment.

Possible responses:
• renegotiate supply contracts;
• source alternative suppliers;
• improve productivity;
• redesign products;
• reduce waste;
• adjust prices;
• offer smaller pack sizes;
• improve value communication.

Evaluation:
Passing all cost increases to customers may protect margins but could reduce demand. Absorbing all increases protects market share but may damage profitability. The appropriate response depends on elasticity, competition and brand strength.

SCENARIO 2: EXCHANGE-RATE MOVEMENT

An importer buys stock in foreign currency.

If the local currency depreciates:
foreign-currency purchase × higher local-currency exchange rate = higher local cost.

Effects:
• gross margin pressure;
• higher selling price;
• increased working capital;
• possible lower demand.

Responses:
• local sourcing;
• forward planning;
• shorter inventory cycles;
• revised pricing;
• supplier negotiation;
• product substitution.

SCENARIO 3: POWER INTERRUPTION

Operations are repeatedly interrupted.

Effects:
• idle labour;
• lower capacity utilisation;
• missed deliveries;
• spoilage;
• higher generator/fuel costs.

Possible responses:
• backup generation;
• solar/alternative energy;
• production scheduling;
• preventive maintenance;
• contingency suppliers.

Evaluation:
A backup system has a capital cost. The investment should be compared with expected avoided losses and strategic reliability benefits.

SCENARIO 4: NEW REGULATION

A new regulation increases compliance requirements.

A good business response is not simply to resist the regulation. Assess:
• legal obligation;
• compliance cost;
• operational changes;
• customer impact;
• reputational benefit;
• implementation timeline.

CASE-STUDY PRINCIPLE
External factors create both opportunities and threats. A strong answer explains the transmission mechanism from external change to business performance.

HORIZONTAL INTEGRATION

Two firms at the same production stage combine.

Potential benefits:
• larger market share;
• economies of scale;
• reduced duplicated costs;
• stronger bargaining power.

Risks:
• competition concerns;
• culture clashes;
• integration costs;
• management complexity.

VERTICAL BACKWARD INTEGRATION

A manufacturer acquires a supplier.

Benefits:
• supply security;
• control over quality;
• possible lower input cost.

Risks:
• capital requirement;
• reduced flexibility;
• management distraction.

VERTICAL FORWARD INTEGRATION

A producer acquires distribution or retail capacity.

Benefits:
• greater control over customer access;
• distribution margin;
• direct customer information.

Risks:
• unfamiliar activities;
• increased capital requirement;
• channel conflict.

DIVERSIFICATION

Entering a new product and market area.

Potential benefit:
Risk diversification and new growth.

Major risk:
Lack of knowledge and capabilities in both the product and market.

INTERNAL GROWTH

Advantages:
• gradual;
• greater control;
• lower integration risk.

Disadvantages:
• slower;
• may allow competitors to grow faster;
• may require significant retained finance.

EXTERNAL GROWTH

Advantages:
• rapid expansion;
• access to established customers/assets.

Disadvantages:
• acquisition cost;
• integration difficulties;
• possible debt burden.

ECONOMIES OF SCALE
Purchasing economies — bulk buying.
Technical economies — efficient machinery.
Managerial economies — specialist managers.
Financial economies — potentially better access to finance.
Marketing economies — promotion spread across greater output.

DISECONOMIES
As a business becomes very large:
• communication may become slower;
• coordination becomes difficult;
• bureaucracy can increase;
• employees may feel less connected.

GROWTH DECISION
Before expanding, test:
market demand + finance + management capacity + operational capacity + risk + stakeholder impact.

WORKFORCE PLANNING

Demand forecasting:
How many employees and which skills will be needed?

Supply forecasting:
What employees and skills will be available?

GAP ANALYSIS:
Future requirement − expected internal supply = recruitment/development gap.

SUCCESSION PLANNING
Identifies employees who may fill key roles in the future and prepares them through training and experience.

RECRUITMENT SOURCES

Internal:
Promotion, transfer, internal vacancy notices.

External:
Advertising, recruitment agencies, professional networks, colleges and universities.

SELECTION TESTS
Possible tests:
• aptitude;
• technical;
• literacy/numeracy;
• work sample;
• situational judgement.

INTERVIEWS
Structured interviews improve comparability between candidates when questions and scoring criteria are consistent.

INDUCTION
A good induction can reduce early turnover by explaining:
• duties;
• policies;
• safety;
• organisational culture;
• reporting relationships.

TRAINING NEEDS ANALYSIS
Compare required skills with existing employee capability.

TRAINING EVALUATION
Ask whether training changed:
• knowledge;
• behaviour;
• productivity;
• quality;
• safety;
• business results.

APPRAISAL
An appraisal should use clear criteria and evidence rather than personal bias.

DISCIPLINE
A fair disciplinary system should:
• state expectations;
• investigate facts;
• give the employee an opportunity to respond;
• apply consistent procedures;
• follow relevant law/policy.

EQUAL OPPORTUNITY
Recruitment and promotion should be based on relevant criteria and comply with applicable law.

EMPLOYEE WELLBEING
Working conditions affect motivation, safety, absenteeism and retention.

HR METRICS
• labour turnover;
• absenteeism;
• cost per hire;
• time to fill vacancies;
• productivity;
• training cost;
• internal promotion rate.

EXAM TIP
Do not treat HR as an isolated department. Link people decisions to productivity, quality, costs, customer service and strategy.

CUSTOMER VALUE

Customer value can be considered as perceived benefits relative to total customer cost.

Total customer cost may include:
• price;
• travel;
• waiting time;
• delivery cost;
• inconvenience;
• risk.

Therefore, a firm can create value without being the cheapest by improving convenience, quality or service.

POSITIONING MAPS

A positioning map can compare brands on dimensions such as:
• price;
• quality;
• convenience;
• sophistication.

The axes should reflect dimensions that matter to the target customer.

PROMOTION OBJECTIVES
• inform;
• persuade;
• remind;
• build brand awareness;
• generate leads;
• stimulate trial.

PUSH STRATEGY
Promote through intermediaries so they stock and promote the product.

PULL STRATEGY
Create consumer demand so customers request the product from retailers.

PERSONAL SELLING
Useful for complex, expensive or customised products where interaction matters.

SALES PROMOTION
Examples:
• discounts;
• coupons;
• bundles;
• competitions;
• loyalty incentives.

Risk:
Frequent discounts may train customers to wait for promotions and weaken premium positioning.

PUBLIC RELATIONS
Builds reputation and relationships rather than relying solely on direct sales messages.

DIGITAL METRICS
• reach;
• impressions;
• click-through rate;
• conversion rate;
• engagement;
• customer acquisition cost;
• return on advertising spend.

CONVERSION RATE
Conversions / relevant visitors or leads ×100.

CUSTOMER ACQUISITION COST
Marketing and sales cost / number of acquired customers.

RETENTION
Retaining existing customers can be less costly than constantly acquiring new customers, but this depends on the industry and customer economics.

MARKETING ETHICS
Avoid:
• misleading claims;
• hidden charges;
• deceptive comparisons;
• manipulation of vulnerable customers;
• misuse of personal data.

EXAM EVALUATION
The best promotional channel depends on customer behaviour, product complexity, budget, objectives and measurability.

OPERATIONS PLANNING

Operations planning answers:
• what to produce;
• how much;
• when;
• where;
• with which resources;
• to what quality standard.

CAPACITY PLANNING

If forecast demand is 12,000 units and current capacity is 10,000, the business has a capacity gap of 2,000 units.

Options:
• overtime;
• extra shifts;
• subcontracting;
• equipment purchase;
• process improvement;
• new premises.

Each option has cost, quality and risk implications.

BOTTLENECK
A bottleneck is a process stage that limits total throughput.

Example:
Cutting can process 1,000 units/day.
Assembly can process 700.
Packaging can process 900.
Assembly is the bottleneck.

Increasing packaging capacity alone will not increase total output while assembly remains at 700 units/day.

PRODUCTIVITY IMPROVEMENT
Can come from:
• training;
• better layout;
• automation;
• maintenance;
• improved scheduling;
• reduced waste;
• better quality at source.

LABOUR PRODUCTIVITY
If 20 workers produce 2,000 units:
100 units per worker.

After process improvement, 20 workers produce 2,400:
120 units per worker.
Productivity increases by 20%.

DOWNTIME
Causes:
• equipment failure;
• power supply;
• material shortage;
• poor scheduling;
• quality rework.

PREVENTIVE MAINTENANCE
Planned maintenance can reduce unexpected breakdowns, although it consumes time and resources.

OPERATIONS AND SUSTAINABILITY
Reduce:
• energy waste;
• water use;
• packaging waste;
• rejected products;
• unnecessary transport.

SUSTAINABLE OPERATIONS can also reduce cost if resource efficiency improves.

EXAM CONCLUSION
Operations decisions should balance cost, quality, speed, flexibility, reliability and sustainability rather than maximise output alone.

CAPITAL EXPENDITURE

Capital expenditure creates or improves long-term productive capacity.

Examples:
• machinery;
• vehicles;
• buildings;
• major equipment.

REVENUE EXPENDITURE

Routine expenditure supporting current operations:
• wages;
• utilities;
• routine repairs;
• advertising.

WORKING CAPITAL MANAGEMENT

Key components:
• cash;
• inventory;
• receivables;
• payables.

RECEIVABLES STRATEGY
Relaxing credit terms may increase sales but also:
• increases funds tied up;
• increases bad-debt risk;
• delays cash collection.

TIGHTER TERMS may improve cash but reduce sales.

INVESTMENT APPRAISAL

PAYBACK:
Measures how quickly initial cash investment is recovered.

ADVANTAGES:
• simple;
• useful for liquidity/risk.

LIMITATIONS:
• ignores cash flows after payback;
• simple payback ignores time value of money.

ARR:
Uses accounting profit rather than cash flow.
Useful for accounting performance comparison but affected by depreciation and accounting policies.

NPV:
Discounts future cash flows and subtracts initial investment.
A positive NPV generally indicates value creation at the chosen discount rate.

RISK ADJUSTMENT
Where future cash flows are uncertain, management may:
• use sensitivity analysis;
• create scenarios;
• adjust discount rate where appropriate;
• use probability-weighted estimates.

SCENARIO ANALYSIS
Best case:
strong demand, favourable costs.

Base case:
most reasonable assumptions.

Worst case:
weak demand, high costs, disruption.

FINANCE DECISION
The cheapest source of finance is not always best. Consider:
• repayment risk;
• control;
• security;
• flexibility;
• maturity;
• cash-flow matching.

EXAM TIP
If a project has positive NPV but creates a severe liquidity problem before cash inflows arrive, the business still needs to address financing and cash-flow timing.

CORPORATE GOVERNANCE

Corporate governance concerns how organisations are directed, controlled and held accountable.

KEY PRINCIPLES
• accountability;
• transparency;
• responsible decision-making;
• oversight;
• protection of stakeholder interests;
• appropriate internal controls.

BOARD OVERSIGHT
Boards provide strategic oversight and monitor management.

CONFLICTS OF INTEREST
Managers/directors may have personal interests that conflict with company interests. Disclosure and independent review help reduce risk.

FRAUD RISKS
• theft;
• false invoices;
• manipulation of records;
• bribery;
• unauthorised payments;
• inventory diversion.

ANTI-FRAUD CONTROLS
• segregation of duties;
• authorisation limits;
• reconciliations;
• audits;
• whistleblowing channels;
• access controls;
• surprise checks.

WHISTLEBLOWING
A safe reporting mechanism can help employees report misconduct, subject to applicable law and organisational procedures.

BRIBERY
Bribery can:
• distort competition;
• create legal risk;
• damage reputation;
• increase costs;
• undermine trust.

CUSTOMER PROTECTION
Businesses should provide accurate information and safe products/services.

SUPPLY-CHAIN ETHICS
Businesses should consider:
• labour conditions;
• environmental practices;
• responsible sourcing;
• product traceability.

GREENWASHING
Making environmental claims that exaggerate or misrepresent actual performance.

ETHICAL DECISION TEST
Ask:
1. Is it legal?
2. Is it honest?
3. Who could be harmed?
4. Is the decision consistent with stated values?
5. Could it be defended publicly?
6. Does it create long-term risk?

ETHICS AND PROFIT
Ethics does not necessarily mean sacrificing profit. Trust, quality, compliance and reputation can support long-term value.

EXAM EVALUATION
A business should not justify unethical behaviour solely because it is profitable in the short term. Consider legal, reputational, stakeholder and sustainability consequences.
